Z||000000||lou_dias||04-27-2001||01:04 PM||||I just bought an 88GT w/5 speed...a week ago...<BR>The clutch shifted fine at first but now I have over an inch of travel before I feel resistance.  Actually I think I can pull the pedal up an inch...<P>Any, the fluid is really rusty...  I was a little low on clutch fluid.  I bled the clutch a little, no air came out, just rusty fluid.  I know I probably just have to keep bleeding it until the air from one end comes out the other but do I have anything to worry about with the dirty fluid?||||reg||1
Z||000001||1FST2M6||04-27-2001||02:12 PM||||craptastic fluid will kill the seals int eh master and the slave giving you the symptoms you have... NAPA has a rebuils kit if you have a caliper hone and an hour to rebild them... ||||reg||1
Z||000002||perks88gt||04-27-2001||02:32 PM||||Yes, dirty fluid will kill your seals in any hydraulic system, including your brakes, so check that also.  I would advise not to rebuild the cylinders in your clutch system because the actual metal cylinders themselves may be damaged from the dirty fluid.  I rebuilt my slave cylinder and it continualy gave me problems because fluid was leaking by the seals through the cracks on the cylinder wall.  ||||reg||13
Z||000003||lou_dias||04-28-2001||11:23 PM||||Funny, sometimes it will shift just fine...<BR>So if I had to replace 1 first, would it be the master or the slave?  Just to see if it gets better...||||reg||1
Z||000004||fierohoho||04-29-2001||08:59 PM||||I'd replace the master first, it does all the work. A leak at the master means the slave can't do all it should. <P>I would flush all the old fluid out also. DOT3 brake/clutch fluid absorbes moisture and it sounds like yours has plenty.<P>Hmmm, a master does the work for the slave?||||reg||1
